; Tutorial_9_Conclusion.inix -- what to carry away, the words DbDo uses, and where to get help.

[about]
Title=9 - Conclusion
Intro=What to carry away, the words DbDo uses, and the help built in.
Setup=Nothing is needed; this walk is listened to.
Homework=Open the template nearest your own life, and make it yours.

[step]

Say=To finish: what to carry away, the words DbDo uses, and where to get help. First, the summary. A table is a list you move through by row and column, and every question about where you are is a Shift key that changes nothing.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Keys follow words, so a key can be guessed. I name a word; the reader gives the key.
Key=
Hear=Open: Control plus O
Hear=Order: Alt plus O
Hear=Filter: Control plus F
Hear=Play: Alt plus Shift plus P

[step]

Say=Records lead to others.
Key=
Hear=Say Related: Shift plus R
Hear=Into the related records: Alt plus Right Arrow
Hear=Back: Alt plus Left Arrow

[step]

Say=The templates are a start, not a museum. Make them yours; when DbDo improves one, Help, Update from Template keeps your copy current.
Key=
Hear=RadioTrail, to listen
Hear=JobTrail, to search for work
Hear=BookTrail, to read
Hear=MusicTrail, to explore

[step]

Say=Now the words, in alphabetical order. I say the term; the reader says what it means.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=filter.
Key=
Hear=A condition that hides the records not matching it, until it is cleared.

[step]

Say=link.
Key=
Hear=A row in the maps table that ties one record to another, with a kind, such as recorded or member of.

[step]

Say=mark.
Key=
Hear=A flag on a record, for a later command to act on; Control plus M sets it, and Shift reverses it.

[step]

Say=order.
Key=
Hear=The sort of the list, by a column, ascending or reversed.

[step]

Say=pick list.
Key=
Hear=A field's fixed values, offered by Control plus F2, so nothing is mistyped.

[step]

Say=prime.
Key=
Hear=The key DbDo builds from a record itself, by which links and merges find it.

[step]

Say=query.
Key=
Hear=A question in SQL, answered as a table.

[step]

Say=record.
Key=
Hear=One row: one station, one job, one book.

[step]

Say=related records.
Key=
Hear=The records a record leads to, through a link or a shared key.

[step]

Say=report.
Key=
Hear=A saved layout that writes records as a document.

[step]

Say=Say key.
Key=
Hear=Shift and a letter: it asks and changes nothing.

[step]

Say=script.
Key=
Hear=A saved question beside a database, run with Control plus Shift plus I.

[step]

Say=SQLite.
Key=
Hear=The database format DbDo opens: one file, with its tables inside.

[step]

Say=template database.
Key=
Hear=A ready-made database that comes with DbDo, opened with Alt plus Shift plus D; you work in your own copy of it.

[step]

Say=Last, more information. Help is built in. The guide, and what changed.
Key=
Hear=F1, the guide
Hear=Shift plus F1, History

[step]

Say=The version, and what any key does without doing it.
Key=
Hear=Alt plus F1, About
Hear=Control plus F1, Key Describer

[step]

Say=Every command in one list, and these walks again: Alt plus F10, and Play Tutorials on the Help menu.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=DbDo dot md, the guide, explains every command and the ideas behind it; Hotkeys dot md lists every key three ways; and the DbDo page on GitHub, at github dot com slash JamalMazrui slash DbDo, has the newest version.
Key=
Hear=
